    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, but the \"unaided memory\" bit is what's tripping me up. how do you actually prove it's in my \"unaided memory\" versus me just having stolen or copied their specific proprietary code? like, what's the practical line between a general skill and an actual breach of this clause?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proving that knowledge exists in your \"unaided memory\" versus stolen code is notably difficult for a company because they cannot easily distinguish your innate abilities from the specific secrets you absorbed without detailed documentation before and after your employment. In practice, the line is usually drawn by whether the new application is a direct replication of their proprietary methods or if it represents a generalizable problem-solving approach that only happens to seem similar. While the memory clause seems to work against you by creating a legal barrier to reusing skills, legal standards often imply that you cannot simply memorize and retain core secrets; instead, courts typically protect the provider's actual confidential data while assuming you cannot keep that data in your mind indefinitely.",
